      "text": "The given inequality is $\\frac{x+6}{x^2+2x+7} \\ge 0.$\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy this inequality, we need to consider two cases:\n1) When $x$ is in between -6 and -7, i.e., $-6\\le x\\le -7$\n2) When $x$ is greater than -7, i.e., $x > -7$\nFirst, let's look at the second case.\nIf $x$ is greater than -7, the denominator $x^2+2x+7$ will always be positive.\nNext, let's consider the first case.\nThe numerator $x+6$ can be positive or negative depending on the value of $x$.\nThe numerator will be negative when $x<-6$ occurs.\nWe want the expression to be greater than or equal to 0, so if $x<-6$ occurs, we can ignore this case.\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality can be written as $x\\in[-6,-7]$.\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{[-6,-7]}$.",
