      "text": "To find the answer, we will examine the given inequality and determine its interval notation for values of \\(x\\) that satisfy it. \n\nSteps to solve the problem:\n\n1. Factor out the greatest common factor from the denominator.\n2. Determine the critical points by analyzing foci and solid curvers.\n3. Determine whether the inequality holds for \\(x\\) within the interval.\n4. Find the intervals that satisfy the inequality.\n\nSo, let's implement this rationale in the solution below:\n\nStep 1: Factor out the greatest common factor from the denominator.\n\nTo factor out the greatest common factor, we first see that the numerator can be factored as \\(x + 6\\). The denominator is a quadratic equation given as \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\). Since \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has a positive leading coefficient, it is always positive for all real numbers \\(x\\). Hence, it is not part of the inequality that restricts \\(x\\).\n\nHence, the inequality \\( \\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0 \\) simplifies to \\( \\frac{x + 6}{-} > 0 \\) where the denominator is always positive.\n\nWe want to find the intervals where the inequality above holds:\n\nStep 2: Determine the critical points by analyzing focus and solid curves.\n\nFirstly, let's identify the critical points for this inequality by analyzing the critical points based on the numerator's sign. \n\n- The critical points occur where the numerator is equal to zero: \\(x + 6 = 0\\) which gives \\(x = -6\\).\n  \nThus, check values around this critical point to determine the intervals where \\(f(x)\\) is non-negative. \n\nLet's consider all the points that make the inequality:\n\n- \\(x = -6 \\text{ (critical point) }\\)\n- \\(x < -6\\)\n- \\(x > -6\\)\n\nThus, we need to analyze what happens for these intervals:\n\n- \\(x < -6 \\text{ } \\text{(negative numerator, negative denominator, thus positive output)} \\Rightarrow \\text{ holds} \\)\n- \\(x > -6 \\text{ } \\text{(0 numerator, positive denominator, thus non-positive input) } \\Rightarrow \\text{ does not hold} \\)\n\nNow, move on to step 3:\n\nStep 3: Determine whether the inequality holds for \\(x\\) within the interval.\n\nAs per the interval analysis above, the only interval where the inequality \\( \\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0 )\\) holds is for \\(x < -6\\):\n\nStep 4: Find the intervals that satisfy the inequality.\n\nThe valid interval or region where the inequality holds would therefore be our answer often described using interval notation:\n\n\\[\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6)}\\]\n\nThis solution results from considering the critical points and analyzing the sign of the expression in different intervals. Using advanced calculus methods or software could make this process more efficient, but the described step-by-step approach is a clear method to solve similar problems.\n\nTherefore, the solution can be simplified as that the inequality \\( \\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0 \\) holds only for \\(x < -6\\), described in interval notation by:\n\n\\[\\boxed{(-\\infty, -6)}\\]\n\nThus, the values of \\(x\\) for which this inequality holds true place between the critical point \\(-6\\) at the left side and negative infinity at the right side of the function. Importantly, the negative infinity end of the interval represents where the expression approaches \\(-\\infty\\), merging into the left side of the critical point. Each analysis leads to the conclusion shown.\n\nFinally, consolidate the intervals either numerically (numbers) or graphically (symbolic calculations) to read easily.\n\nSo this problem proves extreme observation and thinking is vital to solving more complex ones. Understanding the limitations of elementary algebraic techniques alongside more sophisticated mathematical rigor can invariably yield fruitful results in such problems.",
