      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The inequality can be rearranged to \\[\\dfrac{(x-1)(x+ 7)(x + 6)}{(x - 1)^2 (x + 7)} \\ge 0\\]using the fact that \\[(x+6) > 0.\\]This gives us the two inequalities\n\\[\\dfrac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0;\\]and\\[\\dfrac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} = 0.\\]For the rational function, the simplest way to determine intervals in which the numerator is greater than zero is to find the roots of the numerator and make intervals based on them. In this case, since we have one real root at $-6$ and a multiple root at $-1,$ we must carefully analyze what happens at those two points. Since $(x-1)$ does not divide the denominator, those roots of the numerator do not affect whether the function is positive. At $-6$ and $-1,$ the numerator is positive -- the \"zeroes\" behave in the same way as the same-definite intevals -- but the denominator is negative. This gives us two special cases for the numerator being strictly positive: $(-\\infty,-7]$ and $[-6,-1)\\cup (1, \\infty).$ We note that for all other values of $x$ except $-1,$ the numerator of the rational expression is positive, but the denominator is negative, so the rational expression is negative for those values of $x.$ This means all values of $x\\neq -6, -1,$ and $-1.$ Combining this with the first inequality, we give the solution interval of \\[\\boxed{(-\\infty,-7] \\cup [1, \\infty)}.\\]Rewrite the inequality as \\[(x+6)\\left(\\frac{1}{x^2+2x+7}\\right) \\ge 0.\\]Now, this expression is positive for $x=-6$ and $x=1$. We need to look at where $\\frac{1}{x^2+2x+7}$ is negative. But if $x^2+2x+7$ is positive, $\\frac{1}{x^2+2x+7}$ is also positive. Hence, the inequality is $\\frac{x+6}{x^2+2x+7} \\ge 0$ if $x\\leq -7$ and if $x > 1$. We can conclude that the solution to the inequality is $(\\boxed{(-\\infty,-7]}\\cup [1, \\infty)).$",
